.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-DB 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-db
.Nd record machines, nodes, clusters and deployments in the kldload state database
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm init
.Nm
.Cm vm-register
.Fl -name Ar name
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Cm vm-status
.Fl -name Ar name
.Fl -status Ar status
.Op Fl -ip Ar address
.Nm
.Cm vm-mesh
.Fl -name Ar name
.Fl -mesh-id Ar id
.Fl -wg-pubkey Ar key | Fl -clear
.Nm
.Cm vm-show | vm-delete
.Fl -name Ar name
.Nm
.Cm node-register
.Fl -hostname Ar host
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Cm node-status
.Fl -hostname Ar host
.Fl -status Ar status
.Nm
.Cm node-delete
.Fl -hostname Ar host
.Nm
.Cm cluster-upsert
.Fl -name Ar name
.Op Fl -template Ar t
.Op Fl -status Ar s
.Op Fl -data Ar json
.Nm
.Cm event
.Fl -type Ar t
.Op Fl -subject Ar s
.Fl -message Ar m
.Op Fl -data Ar json
.Nm
.Cm deploy-start
.Fl -playbook Ar p
.Op Fl -cluster Ar c
.Op Fl -target Ar t
.Op Fl -extras Ar json
.Nm
.Cm deploy-finish
.Fl -id Ar n
.Fl -rc Ar rc
.Op Fl -status Ar s
.Op Fl -log Ar path
.Nm
.Cm dump
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The state database,
.Pa /var/lib/kldload/state.db ,
is kldload's record of its estate: the virtual machines it made, the
hosts that are cluster nodes, the clusters, the Ansible runs and a log of
events.
.Xr kldload-inventory 8
turns it into Ansible's inventory, and the web console,
.Xr kld 1
and
.Xr kldload-estate 8
read it.
.Nm
is how the tools that make and remove machines \(em
.Xr kvm-create 8 ,
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
.Xr kvm-delete 8 ,
.Xr kube-cluster 8 ,
.Xr kldload-networks 8
and others \(em write to it.
.Pp
The database holds the schema itself: every connection creates any
missing table and adds any missing column first, so the first tool to use
it, whichever it is, sets it up.
Run as root,
.Nm
also makes the database and its directory writable by the
.Ql kldload
group, so tools running as a member can write too.
.Pp
The register verbs are upserts: a row that exists is updated, and an
option left empty never overwrites what is there.
Deleting is soft: a deleted row keeps its history, is hidden from the
inventory, and comes back if the name is registered again \(em as a new
machine, without the old one's mesh identity.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm init
Create the database and its tables, and report them.
.It Cm vm-register Fl -name Ar name
Record a virtual machine.
.Bl -tag -width "--default-role r" -compact
.It Fl -cluster Ar c
a cluster id or name; a new name makes an empty cluster
.It Fl -role Ar r
its role, set on every call
.It Fl -default-role Ar r
its role only if the row is new
.It Fl -ip Ar a
.It Fl -cpu Ar n , Fl -ram-gb Ar n , Fl -disk-gb Ar n
.It Fl -golden-src Ar g
the golden it was cloned from
.It Fl -status Ar s
.El
.It Cm vm-status Fl -name Ar name Fl -status Ar s Op Fl -ip Ar a
Set a machine's state, and its address if
.Fl -ip
is given; an empty
.Fl -ip
clears it, which takes the machine out of the inventory.
.It Cm vm-mesh Fl -name Ar name Fl -mesh-id Ar id Fl -wg-pubkey Ar key
Record the machine's WireGuard identity, which
.Xr kvm-delete 8
uses to take it off the mesh;
.Fl -clear
removes it.
.It Cm vm-show Fl -name Ar name
Print the machine's row as JSON.
.It Cm vm-delete Fl -name Ar name
Mark the machine deleted.
.It Cm node-register Fl -hostname Ar host
Record a host that is a member of a cluster:
.Fl -role , -cluster , -status , -ip-mgmt , -ip-wg0 , -ip-wg1 , -wg-pubkey .
.It Cm node-status , Cm node-delete
As for machines.
.It Cm cluster-upsert Fl -name Ar name
Record or update a cluster.
.It Cm event
Append a line to the event log.
.It Cm deploy-start
Open a record of an Ansible run and print its number.
.It Cm deploy-finish Fl -id Ar n Fl -rc Ar rc
Close it, with the run's exit status, and
.Ql ok
or
.Ql failed
unless
.Fl -status
says otherwise.
.It Cm dump
Every table as JSON.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev KLDLOAD_DB
Use this database instead.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/lib/kldload/state.db
The database, in SQLite's write-ahead-log mode.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Done.
.It 1
A database error; or, for
.Cm vm-show , vm-status , vm-mesh , node-status
and
.Cm deploy-finish ,
no row of that name or number.
The deletes return 0 for a row that is not there: that is what they ask
for.
.It 2
A usage error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload host on 2026-09-29, against a database made for the
purpose in
.Pa /var/tmp/dbdemo ,
from empty.
The machines named in it did not exist; the database records what it is
told.
.Ss A database, a cluster and two machines (init, cluster-upsert, vm-register)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ export KLDLOAD_DB=/var/tmp/dbdemo/state.db
$ kldload-db init
kldload-db: /var/tmp/dbdemo/state.db ready (9 tables: bob_conversations, clusters, deployments, events, nodes, secrets, services, templates, vms)
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db cluster-upsert --name lab --template k8s-ha --status ready
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db vm-register --name lab-cp-1 --cluster lab --role control_plane --ip 192.168.122.21 --cpu 4 --ram-gb 8 --disk-gb 40 --golden-src k8s-golden --status running
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db vm-register --name lab-w-1 --cluster lab --role worker --ip 192.168.122.31 --status running
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss A cluster node (node-register, node-status)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-db node-register --hostname lab-cp-1 --role control_plane --cluster lab --ip-mgmt 192.168.122.21 --ip-wg0 10.250.0.21 --status ready
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db node-status --hostname lab-cp-1 --status draining
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss A new address, and a misspelt name (vm-status)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-db vm-status --name lab-w-1 --status running --ip 192.168.122.32
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db vm-status --name lab-w-l --status running
kldload-db: no VM named lab-w-l; nothing changed
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Ss The mesh identity, and the row (vm-mesh, vm-show)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-db vm-mesh --name lab-w-1 --mesh-id 31 --wg-pubkey 'k1YxZmFrZS1rZXktZm9yLXRoZS1tYW4tcGFnZS0wMDA9'
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db vm-show --name lab-w-1
{
  "cluster_id": "ae613fd9-5331-4bc1-ab3b-4cac122bb23b",
  "cpu": 2,
  "created_at": "2026-09-30T06:39:10Z",
  "data": "{}",
  "deleted_at": "",
  "disk_gb": 64,
  "golden_src": "",
  "id": "536a09d8-a560-423a-b6b6-0a3015b1f8cf",
  "ip_addr": "192.168.122.32",
  "last_seen": "2026-09-30T06:39:10Z",
  "mesh_id": "31",
  "name": "lab-w-1",
  "node_id": "",
  "ram_gb": 4,
  "role": "worker",
  "status": "running",
  "wg_pubkey": "k1YxZmFrZS1rZXktZm9yLXRoZS1tYW4tcGFnZS0wMDA9"
}
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
The key is not a real one.
.Ss Deleted, then registered again (vm-delete)
The row comes back without the old machine's mesh identity:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-db vm-delete --name lab-w-1
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db vm-show --name lab-w-1
kldload-db: no live VM named lab-w-1
# (exited 1)
$ kldload-db vm-register --name lab-w-1 --cluster lab --status running --ip 192.168.122.33
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db vm-show --name lab-w-1
{
\&...
  "ip_addr": "192.168.122.33",
  "last_seen": "2026-09-30T06:39:11Z",
  "mesh_id": "",
\&...
  "wg_pubkey": ""
}
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss The log (event, deploy-start, deploy-finish)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-db event --type vm --subject lab-w-1 --message "resized to 8 GB"
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db deploy-start --playbook site.yml --cluster lab --target all
1
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-db deploy-finish --id 1 --rc 0 --log /var/log/kldload/ansible-1.log
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Everything (dump)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-db dump
